<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marcus Garvey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1887">1887</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marcus Garvey was a Jamaican political activist who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-negro-improvement-association">Universal Negro Improvement Association</a> in 1914 and led a mass movement of black pride and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism</a>, based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> from 1916. In the early 1920s his organisation negotiated to settle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> in Liberia, but the Liberian government turned against the plan. He was deported from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1927.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey.jpg" alt="Marcus Garvey" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Keystone View Company, restored by Creator:Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marcus_Garvey,_%22Provisional_President_of_Africa%22,_by_the_Keystone_View_Company.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marcus Mosiah Garvey was born on 17 August 1887 in Saint Ann's Bay, in the British colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. His paternal great-grandfather had been born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and his Irish surname came from the family's former enslavers. He was apprenticed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> trade and in 1905 moved to Kingston, where he became the first Afro-Jamaican foreman at the printing division of the P. A. Benjamin Manufacturing Company. In 1910 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>, where in 1911 he launched a bilingual newspaper criticising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a>'s treatment of workers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Garvey returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in July 1914 and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-negro-improvement-association">Universal Negro Improvement Association</a>. In 1916 he moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, lodging in Harlem, and toured 38 states as a speaker. In 1918 he launched the weekly Negro World. He argued that black people should be financially independent of white-dominated societies and set up the Negro Factories Corporation and the Black Star Line, a shipping company of which he became president in 1919. He called for a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and for part of the diaspora to migrate there. His readiness to deal with white racists such as the Ku Klux Klan, who shared his separatism, set him against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a>, who promoted integration.</p><p>In January 1922 he was arrested for mail fraud over the advertised sale of stock in a ship the company did not yet own. He represented himself at his trial in 1923 and was found guilty, while his three co-defendants were acquitted. He served nearly two years in the Atlanta penitentiary, and President Calvin Coolidge commuted his sentence. He was deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in 1927.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In Jamaica Garvey founded the People's Political Party in 1929. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1935, and died there on 10 June 1940, aged 52, after a stroke. His body was returned to Jamaica in 1964, and he was the first person formally recognised there as a National Hero. Critics attacked his collaboration with white supremacists, his violent rhetoric and his prejudice against mixed-race people and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, while others praised him for encouraging pride and self-worth amid poverty, discrimination and colonialism. His ideas influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastafari">Rastafari</a>, the Nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-power-movement">Black Power movement</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marcus Garvey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its early statesmen and scholars, especially Edward Wilmot Blyden, who taught at Liberia College and wrote on African identity, helped shape Pan-Africanism, and <b>Marcus Garvey</b>’s movement looked to Liberia in the early 1920s as a possible homeland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Christian Revivalism, including the Great Revival of 1860–61, Ethiopianism and the Back to Africa ethos, both with 18th-century roots, and the activism of <b>Marcus Garvey</b> all contributed.</blockquote><a href="/religion/rastafari">Rastafari · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Du Bois echoed Martin Delany's insistence that black people take pride in their blackness, a line of thought also reflected in <b>Marcus Garvey</b>, Alain Locke and the salons of Paulette and Jane Nardal in Paris.</blockquote><a href="/movement/black-consciousness-movement">Black Consciousness Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Marcus Garvey</b> offered a more radical vision.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Some of its key lines are drawn from a speech that the Pan-Africanist <b>Marcus Garvey</b> gave at Menelik Hall in Sydney, Nova Scotia, Canada, in October 1937 and published in his magazine Black Man.</blockquote><a href="/songs/redemption-song">Redemption Song</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Saint Ann's Bay, in what is now <a href="/countries/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. Died in West Kensington, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.44,-77.2&amp;year=1921" aria-label="Saint Ann's Bay on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Marcus Garvey"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6030 5925h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6030 5925h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.44,-77.2&amp;year=1921">What was here in 1921? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
